<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Ordovices</h1><p class="st-meta">50 BCE – 78 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Mountain peoples of north-west Wales who hosted Caratacus' last stand and fought Rome until Agricola crushed them in 77 or 78.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Ordovices held the uplands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snowdonia">Snowdonia</a> and much of mid and north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, a region of small hillforts, stone-walled roundhouses and pastoral farming. They enter written history through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, who records that the British leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> moved his war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> into their territory around 51 after leaving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/silures">Silures</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>No coins, kings or towns are known, and power was probably held by local chiefs controlling upland grazing and fortified settlements. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>, the island of Mona, lay on their borders and was a centre of the druids, whose sanctuary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Suetonius Paulinus</a> attacked in 60 before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica's</a> revolt recalled him. Their resistance made the region one of the last parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> to be pacified.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 77 the Ordovices destroyed a Roman cavalry unit. The new governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-julius-agricola">Agricola</a> answered with a campaign that, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says, nearly wiped out the people, and then seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a>. Their land was held by forts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segontium">Segontium</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caernarfon">Caernarfon</a>, and no civitas of the Ordovices is known in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ordovices.jpg" alt="Ordovices" width="320" height="333"><figcaption>Image: author of source image, plus my additions (myself) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wales.pre-Roman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 BCE – 78 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism, druidism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=14&amp;focus=ordovices" aria-label="Ordovices on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4615 284 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ordovices lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4935 540h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4935 540h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United Kingdom. <a href="/atlas?year=14&amp;focus=ordovices">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

